<releaseinfo>0.12.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
&kathenaeum; is a private, offline Wikipedia library with an AI research assistant. It reads &kiwix; ZIM files,
searches them, answers questions from them with a small language model running on your own computer, keeps research
projects with your notes and documents, and shows offline maps whose places open their Wikipedia articles.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>KDE</keyword>
<keyword>KAthenaeum</keyword>
<keyword>Wikipedia</keyword>
<keyword>Kiwix</keyword>
<keyword>ZIM</keyword>
<keyword>offline</keyword>
<keyword>encyclopedia</keyword>
<keyword>education</keyword>
<keyword>research</keyword>
<keyword>map</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
&kathenaeum; turns Wikipedia, downloaded once as a &kiwix; <acronym>ZIM</acronym> file, into a library on your own
computer. You can read and search it without a connection, and ask it questions in plain language: a small language
model, running locally through &ollama;, writes the answer from the articles, and &kathenaeum; links every sentence to the
article it came from.
</para>

<para>
Nothing you read, search for or ask leaves your computer. There is no account and no cloud service. The only things
&kathenaeum; fetches from the Internet are those you ask for: the &kiwix; catalog of libraries and the libraries you
download, map regions, and a weekly check for newer versions of your libraries, which you can turn off.
</para>

<para>What you can do with it:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><link linkend="reading">Read</link> any Wikipedia library, with its pictures, in your desktop's colours.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><link linkend="search">Search</link> every library at once, by title or by the text of every article.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><link linkend="ask">Ask</link> questions and get answers with citations, from all your libraries or from one article.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Keep <link linkend="projects">research projects</link>: articles, saved passages, notes, chats, and documents written in &kate;'s editor, with AI drafts.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Browse <link linkend="maps">offline maps</link>, and click a city, a building or a lake to read about it.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Manage your libraries in the <link linkend="library-manager">Library Manager</link>: download, update, add and remove them.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
&kathenaeum; is the desktop version of Athenaeum, a web application for a home server. It is built for laptops: it needs no
graphics card, and it never builds an index before you can use a library.
</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="getting-started">
<title>Getting Started</title>

<sect1 id="first-library">
<title>Your First Library</title>
<para>
When you start &kathenaeum; for the first time it has no libraries. Choose <guilabel>Library Manager</guilabel> in the
sidebar, then the <guilabel>Catalog</guilabel> tab. The catalog lists every library &kiwix; publishes; it starts on
Wikipedia libraries. Each comes in up to three versions:
</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Introductions only</guilabel> (<quote>mini</quote>): the first part of each article. Small, and enough for many questions.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Full text, no pictures</guilabel> (<quote>nopic</quote>).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Full, with pictures</guilabel> (<quote>maxi</quote>): the largest. The whole English Wikipedia is about 120 GB.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>
Start with a small topic library (Chemistry, Astronomy, a person) to try &kathenaeum; out, then download a larger one.
Press <guibutton>Download</guibutton>; the <guilabel>Downloads</guilabel> tab shows its progress. When it finishes,
&kathenaeum; checks that the file is complete and undamaged, and the library appears under <guilabel>Libraries</guilabel>.
</para>
<para>
A ZIM file you already have can be added with <guibutton>Add File…</guibutton> in the Library Manager; it stays where it is.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ollama">
<title>Setting Up the AI Assistant</title>
<para>
Reading, searching and maps work without anything else. Asking questions needs &ollama;, which runs language models on
your computer. It is not part of Debian: install it from <ulink url="https://ollama.com">ollama.com</ulink>, then download
the two models &kathenaeum; uses by default:
</para>
<screen><userinput><command>ollama</command> pull qwen3.5:2b</userinput>
<userinput><command>ollama</command> pull nomic-embed-text</userinput></screen>
<para>
<guilabel>qwen3.5:2b</guilabel> writes the answers; <guilabel>nomic-embed-text</guilabel> finds the passages that match a
question and links the answer's sentences to their sources. Both were chosen by measuring them on a laptop: they run on
a processor or an integrated graphics chip. <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Models</guisubmenu></menuchoice>
shows whether &ollama; is running and has them, and lets you choose others.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="window">
<title>The Main Window</title>
<para>
The sidebar on the left holds a search box and the places of the application, grouped as <guilabel>Read</guilabel>
(<guilabel>Libraries</guilabel>, <guilabel>Search</guilabel>, <guilabel>Map</guilabel>) and <guilabel>Research</guilabel>
(<guilabel>Ask</guilabel>, <guilabel>Chats</guilabel>, <guilabel>Projects</guilabel>), with the
<guilabel>Library Manager</guilabel>, <guilabel>Settings</guilabel>, this handbook and <guilabel>About</guilabel> at the
bottom. The place you are in is highlighted; a number beside <guilabel>Library Manager</guilabel> counts downloads in
progress or library updates available.
</para>
<para>
The header shows the title of the page and, when you have gone deeper (a library, then an article), a back arrow. On a
narrow window the sidebar is hidden; the button at the left of the header opens it.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="reading">
<title>Reading</title>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The Libraries page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="libraries.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Libraries page, with a card for each installed library</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<sect1 id="library-home">
<title>A Library's Home</title>
<para>
<guilabel>Libraries</guilabel> shows a card for each installed library. Open one to see its home: its description, a box
to find an article by title, the articles you read recently, and Wikipedia's own overview articles
(<quote>Outline of…</quote>, <quote>Index of…</quote>, <quote>Glossary of…</quote>, <quote>Timeline of…</quote>,
<quote>List of…</quote>). <guibutton>Show All</guibutton> lists every one of a kind.
</para>
<para>
<guibutton>A to Z</guibutton> lists every article by title, a letter at a time; <guibutton>Random Article</guibutton>
opens one by chance.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>A library's home</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="library-home.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The home of the Wikipedia library: recently read articles and overview articles</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="articles">
<title>Articles</title>
<para>
Articles are shown cleanly, with their pictures and infoboxes, in your desktop's colours (light or dark). Links to other
articles open in the same page; the arrows in the header go back and forward. <guibutton>Contents</guibutton> jumps to a
section. A link to a web site opens in your web browser, never inside &kathenaeum;.
</para>
<para>
Articles use the whole width of the window. If you prefer a narrower column, which is easier to read on a wide screen,
turn off <guilabel>Use the Whole Window</guilabel> in the article's menu (<guibutton>⋮</guibutton>), or
<guilabel>Use the whole window for articles</guilabel> in <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Reading</guisubmenu></menuchoice>.
</para>
<para>
Select text and right-click it for <guimenuitem>Copy</guimenuitem>, <guimenuitem>Save to Project…</guimenuitem> (keep the
passage in a <link linkend="projects">research project</link>) and <guimenuitem>Ask About This</guimenuitem> (ask the
assistant what the article means by it). <guibutton>Ask</guibutton> opens a <link linkend="ask-article">chat about the
article</link> beside it; <guibutton>Add to Project…</guibutton> adds it to a research project. Passages you have saved
are highlighted in the article, with your note when you point at them.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>An article</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="article.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The article Crater Lake, with its infobox</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="search">
<title>Searching</title>
<para>
Type in the search box at the top of the sidebar and press &Enter; (or press &Ctrl;<keycap>F</keycap> to get there). The
<guilabel>Search</guilabel> page lists articles from every library: first those whose titles match, then those whose text
does, each with the passage that matches. The menu beside the search box limits the search to one library.
</para>
<para>
Search finds words, not meanings: <quote>why is the sky blue</quote> finds articles containing those words. To have the
question answered, <link linkend="ask">ask it</link> instead.
</para>
<para>
A library's home page has its own box, which finds articles by title as you type.
</para>
<note><para>
Some libraries have no full-text index; they can be searched by title only. The Library Manager says so beside them.
</para></note>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="ask">
<title>Asking Questions</title>

<sect1 id="answers">
<title>Answers with Sources</title>
<para>
Choose <guilabel>Ask</guilabel>, type a question and press &Enter;. &kathenaeum; finds the articles that bear on it in
your libraries, reads their best passages, and the model writes an answer from them. The first words appear after a few
seconds; <guibutton>Stop</guibutton> ends the answer early and keeps what was written.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>An answer</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="ask.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>An answer with numbered citations and its sources</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>Under the answer:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>
<guilabel>[1]</guilabel>, <guilabel>[2]</guilabel>… after a sentence name the source it matches. Click one to open that
article at the section it comes from. These numbers are computed by &kathenaeum;, which compares each sentence with the
passages the model was given; they are not the model's own claims.
</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>
<guilabel>[?]</guilabel> marks a sentence that no source clearly supports, or a figure that appears in none of them: check
it. It is a hint, not a fact-checker.
</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>
<guilabel>Sources</guilabel> lists the articles the answer was written from. <guibutton>Save to Project…</guibutton> keeps
the answer in a research project.
</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>
If the libraries do not hold the answer, the assistant says it could not find it rather than answering from memory.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="follow-ups">
<title>Follow-up Questions and Chats</title>
<para>
Ask a follow-up in the same chat (<quote>and how deep is it?</quote>): the assistant knows what <quote>it</quote> refers to.
<guibutton>New Chat</guibutton> starts afresh.
</para>
<para>
Every chat is saved with its answers, sources and citations. <guilabel>Chats</guilabel> lists them, newest first: open one
to read it or continue it, or <guibutton>Rename</guibutton>, <guibutton>Delete</guibutton> or
<guibutton>Add to Project…</guibutton> it. A chat about an article, or one held in a project, is marked as such.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ask-article">
<title>Asking About One Article</title>
<para>
<guibutton>Ask</guibutton> in an article's header opens a chat beside it that answers from that article alone, citing its
sections; a citation scrolls the article to the section. These chats are saved with their article, and the panel lists the
earlier ones.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="projects">
<title>Research Projects</title>
<para>
A project gathers what you find on one subject. Choose <guilabel>Projects</guilabel>, then
<guibutton>New Project…</guibutton>, and give it a title and, if you like, a goal: the question you are researching. A
project has three views: <guilabel>Sources</guilabel>, <guilabel>Chat</guilabel> and <guilabel>Documents</guilabel>.
</para>

<sect1 id="project-sources">
<title>Sources and Notes</title>
<para>Add to a project:</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>an article: <guibutton>Add to Project…</guibutton> in its header, or <guibutton>Add Article…</guibutton> in the project;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a passage: select it in an article, right-click, <guimenuitem>Save to Project…</guimenuitem>;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>an answer: <guibutton>Save to Project…</guibutton> under it;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>a whole chat: <guibutton>Add to Project…</guibutton> in <guilabel>Chats</guilabel>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>
Each source can have a note. Notes appear beside the source, on the passage in its article, and in your documents' preview.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="project-chat">
<title>The Project's Chat</title>
<para>
The <guilabel>Chat</guilabel> view answers from the project's own articles and saved passages, with its goal in mind. Turn on
<guilabel>Also search the whole library</guilabel> to let it use other articles too; they are listed apart, as outside the
project.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="documents">
<title>Documents</title>
<para>
<guibutton>New Document</guibutton> creates a Markdown document. It opens in a window of its own with &kate;'s editor
(highlighting, spell checking, search and replace, and everything you can set in <guibutton>Configure Editor…</guibutton>)
beside a live preview. The document is saved as you type.
</para>
<para>
<guibutton>Cite</guibutton> inserts a link to one of the project's articles, or quotes a saved passage with its source.
Links in the preview open the article in the main window. <guibutton>Export…</guibutton> saves the document as a Markdown
file, with a reference list in place of the links (which mean nothing outside &kathenaeum;);
<guibutton>Export…</guibutton> in the project's header exports the whole project.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="drafts">
<title>Drafts Written by the AI</title>
<para><guibutton>Draft with AI</guibutton> writes a new document for you to edit:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Write From Your Instructions…</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>
Give a title and say what the document should cover and how, in as much detail as you like: <quote>A full report on how
Crater Lake formed. Start with Mount Mazama and the eruption, then the caldera and how the lake filled. Formal tone, with a
short conclusion.</quote> Choose a short document (about three sections) or a full report (up to six longer ones), and
whether it may use the whole library. The model plans the sections from your instructions, then writes each one from the
sources that fit it. &kathenaeum; adds the source numbers, <guilabel>[?]</guilabel> on what to check, and a reference
list. A full report takes a few minutes.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Summary of the Project</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>A summary of each of the project's articles (up to fifteen) and an overview of what they say about its goal.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Notes from a chat</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>The questions explored, the findings and the open questions of one of the project's chats.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
<guibutton>Stop</guibutton> ends a draft and keeps nothing. Drafts come from a small model: read them against their
sources.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="maps">
<title>Maps</title>

<sect1 id="map-regions">
<title>Downloading Regions</title>
<para>
Maps are downloaded once and then work offline. In
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Maps</guisubmenu></menuchoice>, choose
<guibutton>Add a region…</guibutton>. Start with the world overview (about 45 MB: every country's coasts, borders, cities
and main roads), then add the countries, states or regions you want: each card shows the region's shape, and
<guibutton>Show Its Parts</guibutton> leads to the regions inside it (North America, then the United States, then
Oregon).
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Choosing a region</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="map-regions.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The continents, each card showing its shape</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>
A region offers two levels of detail, each with its size: <guilabel>Streets and buildings</guilabel>, or
<guilabel>Roads and towns</guilabel>, about a fifth of the size. To know which places have Wikipedia articles,
&kathenaeum; also downloads the region's OpenStreetMap data, reads it once and deletes it. Settings shows each region's
progress, and lets you update a region to the newest map or remove it.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="map-page">
<title>Exploring the Map</title>
<para>
Choose <guilabel>Map</guilabel> in the sidebar. Drag to move, scroll or use the <guibutton>+</guibutton> and
<guibutton>−</guibutton> buttons to zoom, double-click to zoom in. <guilabel>Find a place…</guilabel> finds places by name
in the regions you have.
</para>
<para>
Click a place (a city, a building, a park, a lake) to see what it is. <guibutton>Read</guibutton> opens its Wikipedia
article from your libraries: OpenStreetMap often records which article describes a place, so <quote>Portland</quote>
opens <quote>Portland, Oregon</quote>. <guilabel>In</guilabel> lists what the place lies within (its park, town, county,
state), each with its article. When no article is known, <guibutton>Search the Libraries</guibutton> searches for the
place's name.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>A place on the map</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="map.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Crater Lake clicked on the map, with its article and the areas it lies within</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
<para>
The map opens where you left it. Map data © OpenStreetMap contributors, drawn with tiles from Protomaps.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="library-manager">
<title>The Library Manager</title>

<sect1 id="installed">
<title>Installed Libraries</title>
<para>
The <guilabel>Installed</guilabel> tab lists your libraries. Click one for its details. The bin button removes a library
from &kathenaeum;, and its file too if you choose; chats, notes and projects keep their text, and they find the library
again if you add it back.
</para>
<para>
New libraries go to the folder shown at the top of the tab; <guibutton>Change…</guibutton> picks another (files already
downloaded stay where they are). <guilabel>Rescan Folder</guilabel>, in the menu, picks up ZIM files copied into it.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="catalog">
<title>The Catalog and Downloads</title>
<para>
The <guilabel>Catalog</guilabel> tab lists the &kiwix; libraries, which you can search, filter by kind and size, and sort.
It is saved, so it can be browsed offline. Downloads continue in the background, can be cancelled and resumed, and are
checked when they finish: a damaged file is never added.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The catalog</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="catalog.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The Kiwix catalog in the Library Manager</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="updates">
<title>Library Updates</title>
<para>
&kiwix; publishes new versions of its libraries every few months. <guibutton>Check for Updates</guibutton> in the Library
Manager (or <guibutton>Check Now</guibutton> in
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Library Updates</guisubmenu></menuchoice>) looks in the catalog and says
which of your libraries have a newer version. &kathenaeum; also checks once a week by itself, and tells you only when it
finds one; you can turn this off in Settings.
</para>
<para>
An update shows as <guibutton>Update to …</guibutton> on the library's card and in the Library Manager. It downloads the
new version beside the old one; once it is ready, remove the old one.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="settings">
<title>Settings</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Models</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Whether &ollama; is running, its address (normally <userinput>http://localhost:11434</userinput>), and the models for
answers and for finding passages. If a model is missing, the command to install it is shown. If &ollama; keeps only one
model loaded, so that every question waits while it swaps them, Settings says so and how to change it.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Library Updates</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The weekly check for newer versions of your libraries, and <guibutton>Check Now</guibutton>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Reading</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Articles across the whole window, or in a column of comfortable reading width.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Maps</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The regions you have, adding more, and the folder they are kept in.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Storage</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The folder for downloaded libraries, and where &kathenaeum; keeps its database of chats, projects and settings.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="shortcuts">
<title>Keyboard Shortcuts</title>
<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead><row><entry>Keys</entry><entry>Action</entry></row></thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry>&Ctrl;<keycap>F</keycap></entry><entry>Go to the search box in the sidebar</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>F1</keycap></entry><entry>Open this handbook</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Enter;</entry><entry>Ask the question, or search for the text, that you have typed</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>+</keycap> / <keycap>−</keycap></entry><entry>Zoom the map in or out</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Esc;</entry><entry>Close the place shown on the map</entry></row>
<row><entry>&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>P</keycap></entry><entry>Show or hide the preview in a document's window</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>
<para>A document's window has all of &kate;'s editing shortcuts.</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="privacy">
<title>Privacy and the Network</title>
<para>
&kathenaeum; works offline. What you read, search for, ask and write stays on your computer: questions go to &ollama; on
this computer, never to a service on the Internet, and articles are shown with nothing loaded from the web. It connects
to the Internet only:
</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>to the &kiwix; catalog and its downloads, when you open the Library Manager, download a library, or check for library updates (by default once a week);</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>to Protomaps and Geofabrik, when you add or update a map region.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
<para>
Links to web sites in articles open in your browser, only when you click them.
</para>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter id="troubleshooting">
<title>Questions and Answers</title>
<qandaset id="faq">
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Asking a question says &ollama; cannot be reached.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Start &ollama; (on most systems, <userinput><command>systemctl</command> start ollama</userinput>), then press
<guibutton>Check Again</guibutton> in Settings. Reading and searching work without it.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A model is missing.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Settings shows the command to download it, for example <userinput><command>ollama</command> pull qwen3.5:2b</userinput>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The answer says it could not find anything, although the library has an article on it.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Questions find articles by their words. Name the subject as the article's title does (<quote>What is the boiling point of
ethanol?</quote>), or open the article and ask about it there with <guibutton>Ask</guibutton>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>A library is marked as not Wikipedia-style.</para></question>
<answer><para>
&kathenaeum; can read and search only libraries made the way &kiwix; makes Wikipedia's. Others (such as Stack Exchange or
TED) may open, but their articles cannot be searched or used for answers.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The map is empty.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Download the world overview and a region in
<menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Maps</guisubmenu></menuchoice>. The map shows only what has been
downloaded.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
<qandaentry>
<question><para>Clicking a place shows no article.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Not every place has a Wikipedia article, and the article must be in one of your libraries: a small topic library will not
have it. <guibutton>Search the Libraries</guibutton> searches for the place's name instead.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

